{"birds": [{"name": "American Goldfinch", "sci": "Spinus tristis", "n": 7, "first": "8:59 AM", "last": "11:03 AM", "ts": 1793192354, "mins": [539, 558, 585, 599, 627, 635, 663], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "One of the strictest vegetarians among birds. Goldfinches nest late in summer, when thistle seeds are ripe, and swap bright yellow for olive-brown in winter.", "vox": "sounds/voice/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113053", "snd": "sounds/spinus-tristis.mp3?v=1793113090", "sndby": "Will Sweet, xeno-canto XC453934, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "goldfinch", "cm": 12.0, "img": "cutouts/goldfinch.png", "w": 517, "h": 369, "len": 415.0, "beak": [0.18, 0.15, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "APgAAAH4AAAD/AAAB/wAAAP+AAEB/wAHAf+ADgH/wBwB/8DwAf/h4AH/94AA//8AAH/8ABA/+AAAH/wAAB/+AAA/fhAAeA8AA/gHgAfgB8APAANAPAAAADgAAAA="}}, {"name": "Carolina Chickadee", "sci": "Poecile carolinensis", "n": 29, "first": "8:59 AM", "last": "10:58 AM", "ts": 1793192356, "mins": [539, 541, 542, 543, 543, 545, 547, 548, 582, 583, 596, 600, 610, 611, 611, 617, 618, 620, 623, 628, 629, 630, 632, 641, 647, 648, 656, 656, 658], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Chickadees hide seeds in many separate spots and can remember where for weeks, then dig them back out when food gets scarce.", "vox": "sounds/voice/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113028", "snd": "sounds/poecile-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113078", "sndby": "J.R. Rigby, xeno-canto XC309925, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "chickadee", "cm": 12.5, "img": "cutouts/chickadee.png", "w": 686, "h": 800, "len": 829.0, "beak": [0.78, 0.05, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 37, "b": "AAB+AQAA/8MAAf+DAAP/gwAD/wYAA/8GAAf/PAAH/7wAB//8AA//+AAf//4AH/+/AD//vwA//38Af/98AP//5AD//sAA//3AAf/9gAH//4AD//+AA///gAf//4APg/wADgH8ABwA+AA4APgAeAH4AHAB8ADgA/AAwAeAAMAPAAAAHwAAADwAAAA8AAAAeAAAADAAAA=="}}, {"name": "House Finch", "sci": "Haemorhous mexicanus", "n": 57, "first": "8:59 AM", "last": "10:52 AM", "ts": 1793192388, "mins": [539, 542, 543, 547, 550, 550, 551, 555, 557, 567, 567, 568, 570, 571, 572, 573, 574, 575, 577, 583, 583, 583, 585, 586, 587, 588, 590, 591, 591, 594, 594, 597, 599, 603, 605, 605, 606, 606, 608, 608, 609, 609, 616, 619, 620, 623, 624, 624, 625, 628, 635, 642, 642, 643, 643, 644, 652], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Native to the West, house finches were released on Long Island in 1940 as pet-shop “Hollywood finches” and spread across the East. Redder males are favored by females.", "vox": "sounds/voice/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113005", "snd": "sounds/haemorhous-mexicanus.mp3?v=1793113108", "sndby": "Ted Floyd, xeno-canto XC622745, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "housefinch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/housefinch.png", "w": 800, "h": 581, "len": 662.0, "beak": [0.12, 0.13, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 23, "b": "B+AAAAfwAQoP8AHOH/gB7wf8Af8D/gH/B/8A3wf/AP4H/4zgB//fwAP/zwAD//4AAf/+AAD//AAAf/gAAD/8AA/+/AAH4B4AB+APAB6AD4A4AAeA8AAAAMAAAAA="}}, {"name": "Blue Jay", "sci": "Cyanocitta cristata", "n": 7, "first": "9:00 AM", "last": "10:14 AM", "ts": 1793192409, "mins": [540, 541, 543, 544, 613, 613, 614], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "That blue isn’t a pigment. Tiny structures in the feathers scatter light, which is why a crushed blue feather looks brown. Jays also bury acorns and help spread oak trees.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793112993", "snd": "sounds/cyanocitta-cristata.mp3?v=1793113328", "sndby": "Christopher McPherson, xeno-canto XC691163, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "bluejay", "cm": 28.0, "img": "cutouts/bluejay.png", "w": 385, "h": 369, "len": 474.0, "beak": [0.99, 0.17, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 31, "b": "AAAPgAAAD+AAAA/4AAAH+AAAD/8AAB//AAA//gAAf/AAAP/wAAH/8AAD//AAB//wAB//4AAf/+AAf//gAH//zAD//5wA//94Af+OcAP/8eAD9/fgA/l/gAPgHwAHgD4AHwA4AB8AeAA+AGAAfADgAHgAwADwAAAA4AAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Ruby-crowned Kinglet", "sci": "Corthylio calendula", "n": 6, "first": "9:01 AM", "last": "10:14 AM", "ts": 1793192515, "mins": [541, 565, 571, 574, 611, 614], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its ruby crown stays hidden under olive feathers until the male gets excited, then it flares up. A ruby-crowned kinglet flicks its wings constantly.", "vox": "sounds/voice/corthylio-calendula.mp3?v=1793112988", "snd": "sounds/corthylio-calendula.mp3?v=1793113143", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC104679, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "rubykinglet", "cm": 10.8, "img": "cutouts/rubykinglet.png", "w": 800, "h": 653, "len": 568.0, "beak": [0.09, 0.35, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 26, "b": "AAAAAwAAAAcAAAAGAAAADAAAADgAAAAwB8AAYA/wAOA//ADAH/8BwAf/hwAH/+YAB//8AAP//gAD//+AAf/jAAD/wAAAH4AAAD8AAAB+AAAA8AAAAfAAAA+AAAAeAAAAeAAAAPAAAAA="}}, {"name": "White-throated Sparrow", "sci": "Zonotrichia albicollis", "n": 22, "first": "9:04 AM", "last": "11:06 AM", "ts": 1793192686, "mins": [544, 550, 551, 552, 552, 553, 553, 558, 559, 600, 601, 601, 601, 602, 602, 624, 627, 631, 655, 660, 665, 666], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "White-throats come in two color forms, white-striped and tan-striped, and almost always pair with the opposite form. Listen for “Oh-sweet-Canada, Canada, Canada.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113071", "snd": "sounds/zonotrichia-albicollis.mp3?v=1793113140", "sndby": "Matt Wistrand, xeno-canto XC339648, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "whitethroated", "cm": 17.0, "img": "cutouts/whitethroated.png", "w": 800, "h": 460, "len": 599.0, "beak": [0.73, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 18, "b": "AAB+AAAB/gAAB/8DAB/+BwB//h4B//54f//84P///8AAP/8AAA/8AAAD8AAAA/AAAA8AAAA/AAAAeAAAAeAAAAeAAAAHAAAA"}}, {"name": "Eastern Bluebird", "sci": "Sialia sialis", "n": 51, "first": "9:07 AM", "last": "11:04 AM", "ts": 1793192826, "mins": [547, 548, 548, 548, 549, 551, 557, 564, 565, 565, 565, 569, 569, 570, 578, 578, 579, 579, 586, 586, 586, 587, 591, 592, 613, 615, 616, 616, 616, 620, 621, 621, 622, 623, 625, 625, 625, 626, 626, 627, 628, 628, 629, 629, 630, 630, 631, 631, 635, 664, 664], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Bluebirds nest in tree cavities, and their numbers crashed in the early 1900s when starlings and house sparrows took the holes. Volunteer nest-box trails helped them rebound.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113046", "snd": "sounds/sialia-sialis.mp3?v=1793113130", "sndby": "Robin Carter, xeno-canto XC1374, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "bluebird", "cm": 18.0, "img": "cutouts/bluebird.png", "w": 800, "h": 609, "len": 744.0, "beak": [0.69, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 24, "b": "AAPwAAAH/AAAD/4AAA/4AAAf8AAAH/AAAD/wAAB/+AAAf/gDAP/wDgH/8BwD//B4A//h4Af/54AH/94AD/98AB//4AA/P4AAPH0AAHPhAABvgAAA3gAAAPgAAABwAAAA"}}, {"name": "Tufted Titmouse", "sci": "Baeolophus bicolor", "n": 17, "first": "9:09 AM", "last": "10:25 AM", "ts": 1793192969, "mins": [549, 549, 550, 551, 552, 562, 562, 563, 564, 600, 603, 604, 604, 605, 624, 625, 625], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Titmice line their nests with hair, and they have been seen plucking it from live raccoons, dogs, and even people.", "vox": "sounds/voice/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793112971", "snd": "sounds/baeolophus-bicolor.mp3?v=1793113083", "sndby": "Jim Berry, xeno-canto XC649331, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "titmouse", "cm": 16.5, "img": "cutouts/titmouse.png", "w": 574, "h": 800, "len": 653.0, "beak": [0.2, 0.25, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 45, "b": "cAAAAHAAAAAwAAAAIAAAAGgDAAB8B4AAPB8AAD5/AAA+/4AAeP+AAHv/wAB7/8AA+P/gAPhn4ACbH/AADz/wAA9/+AAPf/wABb/8AB+//gAO//4ABt//AAfP/wAHx/+AB+P/gAPh/8AHiP/AB47/wAeH/+AHofvgBvOB8AD3APgAfAB4APgAPAD4AB4A+AAfAPwADwA3AA8APwAHAD4AAAAfAAAAGwAAABgAAAAMAAAADAAA"}}, {"name": "Red-shouldered Hawk", "sci": "Buteo lineatus", "n": 28, "first": "9:11 AM", "last": "10:31 AM", "ts": 1793193060, "mins": [551, 554, 555, 555, 555, 556, 556, 557, 557, 558, 560, 560, 562, 563, 563, 563, 564, 564, 565, 565, 566, 566, 566, 567, 567, 575, 591, 631], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its loud, repeated kee-ah call is a favorite for Blue Jays to copy, so a hawk you hear in the yard may really be a jay.", "vox": "sounds/voice/buteo-lineatus.mp3?v=1793193504", "snd": "sounds/buteo-lineatus.mp3?v=1793193566", "sndby": "Denis Provencher, xeno-canto XC808846, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "life": true, "key": "redshouldered", "cm": 43.0, "img": "cutouts/redshouldered.png", "w": 523, "h": 800, "len": 863.0, "beak": [0.987, 0.093, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 49, "b": "AAAH+AAAD/4AAA/+AAAf/wAAP/8AAD//AAA/+AAAf/AAAP/4AAH/+AAB//gAA//4AAf/+AAP//gAH//8AD///AB///wAf//4AP//+AD///gB///wAf//8AP//+AD///gA///wAf//8AH//+AD///AA///gAP//4AH//8AB///AAf//wAH//4AB//+AA///gAP//4AH//8AD/9/gA/+f4AP/AewD/g//gPwf/4D4H/4B+B3+AfgAcAHwADAD8AAAAeAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Carolina Wren", "sci": "Thryothorus ludovicianus", "n": 17, "first": "9:17 AM", "last": "10:40 AM", "ts": 1793193444, "mins": [557, 557, 558, 558, 559, 559, 560, 561, 561, 561, 562, 593, 593, 639, 640, 640, 640], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Its song sounds like “teakettle, teakettle, teakettle,” and pairs often sing duets. It will nest in odd places: flowerpots, mailboxes, even old boots.", "vox": "sounds/voice/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113060", "snd": "sounds/thryothorus-ludovicianus.mp3?v=1793113075", "sndby": "Jacob Saucier, xeno-canto XC498646, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "wren", "cm": 13.0, "img": "cutouts/wren.png", "w": 800, "h": 633, "len": 781.0, "beak": [0.85, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 25, "b": "OAAAADgAAAA8AAAAHgB+AB4B/+APA//wB5//AAf//gAH//4AB//+AAf//AAH//wAD//4AAP/+AAB//H/AP/f/wA///8AGf/cAB/7AA//8AAP/4AAP/8AAP9gAAD8AAAAcAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Fish Crow", "sci": "Corvus ossifragus", "n": 14, "first": "9:22 AM", "last": "10:57 AM", "ts": 1793193762, "mins": [562, 566, 586, 591, 600, 612, 613, 613, 624, 625, 626, 656, 657, 657], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It looks just like an American Crow. The giveaway is the voice: a nasal, two-note “uh-uh” instead of a clear “caw.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793112991", "snd": "sounds/corvus-ossifragus.mp3?v=1793113105", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC130581, CC BY-NC-SA 3.0", "key": "fishcrow", "cm": 38.0, "img": "cutouts/fishcrow.png", "w": 800, "h": 474, "len": 557.0, "beak": [0.27, 0.08, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 19, "b": "AH4AAAD+AAAAPwAAAB+AAAAf4AAAH/AAAB/4AAAf/gAAD/4AAAf/AAAD/4AAAf/AAAD34P8Bs+D///Dg/////v////8AD///AAAP/w=="}}, {"name": "Cedar Waxwing", "sci": "Bombycilla cedrorum", "n": 2, "first": "9:22 AM", "last": "9:57 AM", "ts": 1793193766, "mins": [562, 597], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "The red tips on its wing feathers look like drops of sealing wax, which gave waxwings their name. They sometimes pass berries beak to beak along a row of perched birds.", "vox": "sounds/voice/bombycilla-cedrorum.mp3?v=1793112972", "snd": "sounds/bombycilla-cedrorum.mp3?v=1793113168", "sndby": "Rory Nefdt, xeno-canto XC1142128, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "life": true, "key": "waxwing", "cm": 17.0, "img": "cutouts/waxwing.png", "w": 800, "h": 738, "len": 708.0, "beak": [0.61, 0.21, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 30, "b": "AAAADgAAAAwAAAAMAA8ADwAfwB8AD/AfAB/x2AAf4fgAP8H4AH+B/AD/xPwA/8X4Af/HgAP/hwAD/4YAB/+OAA//nAAP/zAAD/5wAB/9wAA//4AAP/8AAH/eAAA8+gAAeHAAAHDgAADj4AAAw4AAAEcAAAAGAAAA"}}, {"name": "Northern Cardinal", "sci": "Cardinalis cardinalis", "n": 3, "first": "9:31 AM", "last": "9:32 AM", "ts": 1793194280, "mins": [571, 572, 572], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Female cardinals sing too, which is unusual for a North American songbird. During courtship the male feeds her seeds beak to beak.", "vox": "sounds/voice/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793112978", "snd": "sounds/cardinalis-cardinalis.mp3?v=1793113085", "sndby": "David Tattersley, xeno-canto XC1000248, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "cardinal", "cm": 22.0, "img": "cutouts/cardinal.png", "w": 516, "h": 348, "len": 530.0, "beak": [0.06, 0.28, "L"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AYAAAAeAAAAPgAAAH4AAAB/AAAA/4AAAf/gAAB/+AAAf/4AAH//AAA//8AAP//gAB//8AAf//AAD//+AAf//wEB/v/jwf3v+///+/z//vn945gA+MAAAAA=="}}, {"name": "White-breasted Nuthatch", "sci": "Sitta carolinensis", "n": 13, "first": "9:34 AM", "last": "11:01 AM", "ts": 1793194491, "mins": [574, 575, 575, 608, 610, 618, 620, 623, 623, 632, 633, 658, 661], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "Nuthatches climb down tree trunks head first. The name comes from “nut-hack”: they wedge a seed into bark and hack it open.", "vox": "sounds/voice/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113050", "snd": "sounds/sitta-carolinensis.mp3?v=1793113095", "sndby": "Andrew Spencer, xeno-canto XC14860, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "nuthatch", "cm": 14.0, "img": "cutouts/nuthatch.png", "w": 532, "h": 327, "len": 372.0, "beak": [0.79, 0.42, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 20, "b": "fgAAAH8AAAB/AAAAP4AAAD+AAAAfwAAAH/4gAB///gAf///AB///gAf//gAD//wAA//4AAP/8AAB//AAAf/AAAD+AAAA/gAAAP4AAAB/AAA="}}, {"name": "Red-bellied Woodpecker", "sci": "Melanerpes carolinus", "n": 2, "first": "9:39 AM", "last": "9:40 AM", "ts": 1793194778, "mins": [579, 580], "anim": null, "trunk": true, "fact": "The “red belly” is only a faint pink blush you will rarely see. The red cap is the giveaway. Its barbed tongue can reach about two inches past the bill tip.", "vox": "sounds/voice/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113012", "snd": "sounds/melanerpes-carolinus.mp3?v=1793113332", "sndby": "Bruce Lagerquist, xeno-canto XC713255, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "redbellied", "cm": 24.0, "img": "cutouts/redbellied.png", "w": 400, "h": 800, "len": 516.0, "beak": [0.52, 0.23, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 64, "b": ""}}, {"name": "Eastern Towhee", "sci": "Pipilo erythrophthalmus", "n": 4, "first": "9:48 AM", "last": "9:49 AM", "ts": 1793195291, "mins": [588, 588, 588, 589], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Towhees scratch through leaf litter with a two-footed hop-and-kick. The song says it all: “drink-your-teeeea.”", "vox": "sounds/voice/pipilo-erythrophthalmus.mp3?v=1793113027", "snd": "sounds/pipilo-erythrophthalmus.mp3?v=1793113162", "sndby": "Ross Gallardy, xeno-canto XC310201, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "life": true, "key": "towhee", "cm": 20.0, "img": "cutouts/towhee.png", "w": 800, "h": 545, "len": 636.0, "beak": [0.64, 0.09, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 22, "b": "AAHwAAAD+AAAB/gAAA/wAAAf8AAAP/ADAH/wBgD/8BwB/+B4Af/g8AP/wcAD/8cAA/8eAAf/+AAfE+AAHh+AAD4fgAB8fAAA+PwAAPPAAAAPAAAADgAAAA=="}}, {"name": "Pileated Woodpecker", "sci": "Dryocopus pileatus", "n": 3, "first": "9:57 AM", "last": "10:04 AM", "ts": 1793195870, "mins": [597, 603, 604], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "It chisels big rectangular holes in dead wood while hunting carpenter ants. Abandoned holes later shelter owls, ducks and other cavity nesters.", "vox": "sounds/voice/dryocopus-pileatus.mp3?v=1793112997", "snd": "sounds/dryocopus-pileatus.mp3?v=1793113171", "sndby": "Mike Nelson, xeno-canto XC102028, CC BY-NC-ND 2.5", "key": "pileated", "cm": 45.0, "img": "cutouts/pileated.png", "w": 649, "h": 800, "len": 737.0, "beak": [0.42, 0.1, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 39, "b": "AAAf/gAAH/4AAB/+Bzwf/x/4H/9/8B///+Af/3/AH/8/8B//D/wf/wf+H/8D/x//Af+f/wP/n/8D/5//A////wH///8B////Af///wH///4A/9/+AP/f/gD/3/4Af5//AH+f/wA/v/4AP7//AB+//wAf//8AD///AAf//wAH//8ABv/+AAD//gAAf/4AAH//AAA//wAAP/8AAD/+"}}, {"name": "American Crow", "sci": "Corvus brachyrhynchos", "n": 3, "first": "10:52 AM", "last": "10:55 AM", "ts": 1793199155, "mins": [652, 652, 655], "anim": null, "trunk": false, "fact": "Crows remember individual human faces for years, and they seem to pass the grudge along to other crows.", "vox": "sounds/voice/corvus-brachyrhynchos.mp3?v=1793112990", "snd": "sounds/corvus-brachyrhynchos.mp3?v=1793113335", "sndby": "David Vander Pluym, xeno-canto XC289011, CC BY-NC-SA 4.0", "key": "crow", "cm": 45.0, "img": "cutouts/crow.png", "w": 553, "h": 364, "len": 568.0, "beak": [0.94, 0.14, "R"], "mask": {"c": 32, "r": 21, "b": "AAAHwAAAD/gAAB/+AAB//gAD/+AAD//AAB//wAB//4AB//+AB///AAf//wAP//4AH//4AH//8AD//8AA/x+AAPgPwAbwF/6/f////3///7Bhw5AA"}}, {"name": "Leuconotopicus villosus", "sci": "Leuconotopicus villosus", "n": 2, "first": "11:06 AM", "last": "11:07 AM", "ts": 179319997